course: world
lesson: 69
title: Wind and the air around the Earth
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: The Earth is wrapped in a thin blanket of air called the atmosphere, held to the planet by gravity. If the Earth were an apple, the air would be thinner than its skin.
explain: The air is thickest near the ground and gets thinner the higher you go. Almost all the weather happens in the lowest layer, the troposphere, which is only about 12 kilometres thick, and in it the air gets colder as you climb, which is why mountain tops are snowy.
explain: Higher up, in the stratosphere, there is a layer of a gas called ozone that soaks up much of the Sun's harmful ultraviolet light. About 100 kilometres up, the air is so thin that space is said to begin.
explain: Wind starts with the Sun: it warms some places more than others, warm air rises, and cooler air flows in to take its place.
explain: For example, on a sunny day at the seaside the land warms faster than the sea, so the air over the land rises and a cool sea breeze blows onto the beach; at night the land cools faster and a land breeze blows out to sea.
fact: The atmosphere is the layer of air around the Earth, held by gravity.
fact: The air gets thinner the higher you go.
fact: High up the thin air presses less on you, so your ears can pop when you go up a high hill or fly in a plane.
fact: Almost all weather happens in the lowest layer of the atmosphere, the troposphere.
fact: In the troposphere the air usually gets colder the higher you go.
fact: The ozone layer in the stratosphere soaks up much of the Sun's harmful ultraviolet light.
fact: Big passenger planes fly about 10 kilometres up, above most clouds and storms.
fact: Space is usually said to begin about 100 kilometres above the ground.
fact: Winds start because the Sun warms the Earth unevenly.
fact: Land warms up and cools down faster than water.
fact: A sea breeze blows from the sea onto the land on sunny days; a land breeze blows out to sea at night.
fact: An anemometer measures how fast the wind blows.
fact: A wind is named after the direction it comes from, so a north wind blows from the north.
exercise: What is the atmosphere? | The layer of air around the Earth | The rocky crust under our feet | The water of all the oceans | The empty space between the planets
exercise: What holds the atmosphere around the Earth? | Gravity | The Earth's magnetism | The clouds | The Moon
exercise: In which layer of the atmosphere does almost all weather happen? | The troposphere, the lowest layer | The stratosphere, the middle layer | The very top, where space begins | All layers have the same weather
exercise: What happens to the air as you go higher in the troposphere? | It gets thinner and usually colder | It gets thicker and warmer as you climb | It gets thicker and colder the higher you go | It stays exactly the same all the way up
exercise: What does the ozone layer do? | It soaks up much of the Sun's harmful ultraviolet light | It makes the rain that falls on the Earth | It gives us the oxygen we breathe | It keeps the Moon in its orbit
exercise: What makes the wind start? | The Sun warming some places more than others | The trees waving their branches back and forth | The Earth's magnetism pulling the air along with it | The waves of the sea pushing the air onto land
exercise: Why does a sea breeze blow onto the beach on a sunny day? | The land warms faster, its air rises and cooler sea air flows in | The sea warms faster and pushes its air onto the land | The waves throw the air up the beach | The Moon pulls the air towards the land
exercise: Which way does a land breeze blow at night? | From the land out to the sea | From the sea onto the land | Straight up into the sky | Along the beach only
exercise: Which warms up faster in the sunshine? | Land | Water | They warm exactly the same | Neither ever warms
exercise: About how high do big passenger planes fly? | About 10 kilometres | About 1 kilometre | About 100 kilometres | About 1,000 kilometres
exercise: About how high up is space usually said to begin? | About 100 kilometres | About 1 kilometre | About 10 kilometres | About 10,000 kilometres
exercise: What does an anemometer measure? | How fast the wind blows | How hot the air is | How much rain has fallen | How high the clouds are
exercise: Where does a north wind come from? | From the north | From the south | It blows towards the north | From high in the sky
exercise: Why are mountain tops often covered in snow even in summer? | The air high up is colder | They are closer to the Moon | The Sun never shines on them | Snow is heavier than the rock
test: A climber goes up a very high mountain. Which is true near the top? | The air is colder and thinner | The air is warmer and thicker | There is no air at all | The air is warmer because it is nearer the Sun
test: A big plane flies over a thunderstorm. How can it do that? | It flies above the layer where most weather happens | Storms are only a few metres high | Planes push the storm aside | Storms only happen at night
test: If the ozone layer grew much thinner, what would happen? | More harmful ultraviolet light would reach the ground | The air would have less oxygen to breathe | It would stop raining everywhere | The sky would turn dark in the daytime
test: On a sunny afternoon you stand on the beach. Which way does the breeze most likely blow? | From the sea onto the land | From the land out to the sea | Straight down from the sky | There is never any breeze by the sea
test: A weather report says the wind is from the west. Which way is a kite pulled? | Towards the east | Towards the west | Towards the north | Straight upwards
test: If the Sun warmed every place on the Earth exactly the same, what would happen to the winds? | There would be much less wind | There would be much more wind | The winds would be just the same | The winds would all blow upwards
test: Which is true about the air around the Earth? | It is a thin layer compared with the size of the Earth | It reaches all the way up to the Moon and a little beyond | It is equally thick at every height | It is held in place by the clouds
test: Why do your ears sometimes pop when you drive up a high hill? | The air pressure gets lower as you go up | The air gets warmer and warmer as you go up | The car engine pushes hard on your ears as it climbs | The sound of the wind outside gets much louder
test: At night the wind at the seaside turns and blows out to sea. Why? | The land cools faster than the sea | The sea cools faster than the land | The Moon pulls the air out to sea | The waves stop moving at night
test: windy cos of what tho, where does wind come from | The Sun warms the Earth unevenly and air moves | The trees wave their branches and push the air | The Earth spinning fast blows the air along | The clouds push the air as they float by
test: we're at the beach, sunny arvo, which way's the breeze blowing | From the sea onto the land | From the land out to the sea | Straight down from the sky | Along the beach and never across it
test: Mia says a north wind blows towards the north. true? | No, a wind is named after where it comes from, so it blows from the north | Yes, winds are named after where they are going | Yes, but only in the Southern Hemisphere | No, a north wind always blows from the east
test: my brother reckons it gets warmer the higher u climb cos ur closer to the sun. right? | No, in the troposphere the air usually gets colder higher up | Yes, being closer to the Sun up there warms the air much more | Yes, but only above the highest clouds | No, the air stays the same at every height
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the troposphere? | The lowest layer of the atmosphere, wherein almost all weather occurs | The layer containing the ozone which absorbs ultraviolet light | The region at which space is said to commence | The uppermost and thinnest layer of the atmosphere
exam: Identify the principal cause of winds upon the Earth. | The uneven warming of the Earth by the Sun | The rotation of the Moon about the Earth | The motion of ships upon the oceans | The magnetism of the Earth's core
exam: It is given that a wind is designated a north wind. Determine the direction thereof. | It blows from the north | It blows towards the north | It blows from the south | It blows only at the North Pole
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning a sea breeze. | It blows from the sea onto the land on sunny days | It blows from the land out to sea on sunny days | It blows only during storms at night | It blows from the land out to sea by day and night alike
